# Model routing policy
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
Codex, acting as Marketing Chief, chooses a capability class before delegation
and remains the final orchestrator. Hermes may execute the selected local or
scheduled route, while Claude, Grok, Cursor, and other models act as bounded
specialists. Model availability and provider health must be verified live;
these preferences do not authorize new accounts, paid routes, or configuration
changes.
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
| Class | Use | Preferred route when healthy | Guardrail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Local/fast | extraction, formatting, classification, status checks | configured local or low-cost route | no unsupported synthesis claims |
| Balanced | routine coding, first drafts, bounded research synthesis | GPT-5.6 Sol-class or mid-tier Grok route | explicit budget and acceptance checks |
| Frontier | strategy, critique, hard refactors, brand-critical judgment | Grok 4.5-class, Claude frontier, or strongest approved Codex route | smallest context slice; max two evaluator loops |
| Image | client visual generation | Grok Imagine when available and approved for the task | verified logo and reference bundle required |
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
## Routing defaults
System.Management.Automation.RemoteException
- Retrieval and formatting do not need a frontier model.
- Strategy and final critique should not use the same cheap worker that produced
  the first draft when independent review materially matters.
- Pass targeted artifacts, not full conversation history.
- Maximum concurrent workers: 3.
- Every step declares `budget_tokens` and `timeout_seconds` before execution.
- The evaluator may return at most two revision loops before escalating a clear
  blocker or decision.
- Review aggregate usage before adding scheduled work. Cron volume is currently
  the first optimization surface.
- Never silently change Hermes, Codex, Claude, Grok, OmniRoute, or provider
  defaults.
